            "question": "The linear equation $2x - 5y = 7$ has",
            "options": [
                "A unique solution",
                "Two solutions",
                "Infinitely many solutions",
                "No solution"
            ],
            "finalAnswer": "(c) Infinitely many solutions",
            "solutionSteps": [
                "A linear equation in two variables represents a straight line.",
                "Every point on the line is a solution, and there are infinite points on a line."
            ]
        },
        {
            "id": "q6",
            "type": "subjective",
            "marks": 3,
            "question": "Evaluate $(999)^3$ using suitable identity.",
            "finalAnswer": "997002999",
            "solutionSteps": [
                "$(999)^3 = (1000 - 1)^3$.",
                "Use $(a-b)^3 = a^3 - b^3 - 3ab(a-b)$.",
                "$(1000)^3 - 1^3 - 3(1000)(1)(1000-1)$",
                "$= 1,000,000,000 - 1 - 3000(999)$",
                "$= 1,000,000,000 - 1 - 2,997,000 = 997,002,999$."
            ]
        },
        {
            "id": "q7",
            "type": "subjective",
            "marks": 3,
            "question": "Factorize: $8x^3 + 27y^3 + 36x^2y + 54xy^2$.",
            "finalAnswer": "(2x + 3y)³",
            "solutionSteps": [
                "Notice the form $a^3 + b^3 + 3a^2b + 3ab^2 = (a+b)^3$.",
                "Here, $a = 2x$ and $b = 3y$.",
                "$(2x)^3 + (3y)^3 + 3(2x)^2(3y) + 3(2x)(3y)^2$",
                "$= (2x+3y)^3$."
            ]
        },
        {
            "id": "q8",
            "type": "subjective",
            "marks": 4,
            "question": "If $x+y+z = 0$, show that $x^3+y^3+z^3 = 3xyz$.",
            "finalAnswer": "Proven",
            "solutionSteps": [
                "We know: $x^3+y^3+z^3 - 3xyz = (x+y+z)(x^2+y^2+z^2-xy-yz-zx)$.",
                "Given $x+y+z = 0$, the RHS becomes $0 \\times (\\dots) = 0$.",
                "Therefore, $x^3+y^3+z^3 - 3xyz = 0 \\implies x^3+y^3+z^3 = 3xyz$."
            ]
        },
        {
            "id": "q9",
            "type": "subjective",
            "marks": 5,
            "question": "Factorize $x^3 - 23x^2 + 142x - 120$.",
            "finalAnswer": "(x - 1)(x - 10)(x - 12)",
            "solutionSteps": [
                "By trial, if $x=1$, $1 - 23 + 142 - 120 = 0$. So $(x-1)$ is a factor.",
                "Dividing $x^3 - 23x^2 + 142x - 120$ by $(x-1)$ gives $x^2 - 22x + 120$.",
                "Factorize quadratic: $x^2 - 12x - 10x + 120 = x(x-12) - 10(x-12) = (x-10)(x-12)$.",
                "Factors are $(x-1)(x-10)(x-12)$."
            ]
        },
